<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Campine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kempen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Campine, or Kempen, is a region of sandy heathland and pine forest in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, roughly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the Demer. In late Roman times it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toxandria">Toxandria</a>, where the Emperor Julian allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> to settle in 358. Poor soils kept it thinly settled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and industry developed in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/campine.jpg" alt="Campine" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mystro82 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeKempen_NL_BE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
